A||||7||RossT||dist. rust! what to clean?||||1||
Z||000000||RossT||10-28-2001||03:48 PM||||Ok, so I took my distr. cap off to gain room to the egr bolts and look what I foud.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image011_3.jpg"> <P>How much of this do I clean up?  Can I clean the whole base and under the module?.  How much dielectric gease do I put back under the module?  Does the base need any dielectric gease on it too.  What about the rest of it?<P>Thanks for you help.<P>This is on a 33,000 mile formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-28-2001||05:43 PM||||If I felt ambitious and had some spare time, I'd pull the distributor, remove the shaft and polish it, clean the baseplate, module and reinstall the module with heatsink compound.<P><B>Edit:</B> Replace pickup coil <B>and</B> the O-ring!! How could I forget those items??<P>Since I'm rarely ambitious   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, I'd remove the module, clean it and the baseplate, apply a smear of heatsink compound, and reinstall the module.  That's heatsink compound, not dielectric grease (maybe they're really the same thing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).  But I'm pretty sure you want heatsink compound - Radio Scrap   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> carries it.   <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||10-28-2001||05:51 PM||||Id pull it and fully clean it, shaft and all.  It should all be clean.  Replace the pick up coil well your in there.<P>ive used dielectric grease in the past and havent had any module failures.  use a glob that is maybe pinky finger nail sized.  You want just enough to coat the base, too much and your going to insulate not promote heat transfer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||10-28-2001||07:00 PM||||Do you only put heat sink compound/dielectric<BR>grease under the module?  or do you also smear in the the base not covered by the module?  The reason I ask is that the base not covered by the module lookes like it has<BR>dielectric geas on it now!<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-28-2001||09:04 PM||||Heat sink compound, at least the stuff most of you've seen, is mainly dielectric grease with Zink Oxide mixed into it. There' are other kinds but they aren't generally available to the public. The heat sink compound is better than plane dielectric. (4x better according to one maker. there's notes in the PC area of my main cave about this.)<P>Clean everything up good. Tip, to remove magnetic dirt from magnetis object... After you are done scrubbing/sanding/whatever, use some of that modling clay that never hardens, "fun tack" picture hanging putty also works.<P>I'd replace the pickup coil for sure. and the O ring if you take it out of the block.<P>Coating the whole base isn't needed but it could help reduce corrosion of the aluminum. Same effect as oiling bare steel. I'm not sure in this case if coating stuff is a good idea or not.<P>BTW... In case there was any doubt, that dist cap and rotor are shot.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||GodSend||10-29-2001||09:28 AM||||For those of us who do not know, exacty which piece is the pickup coil? (I have seen this work thrown around alot, but I am not sure which piece is refers to! ).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-29-2001||11:58 AM||||It's the only coil of wire in there. You can't see it very well in the picture above. it sits inside those prongs that are pointing up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GodSend||10-29-2001||01:48 PM||||Ah, ok, I know what you are talking about.  How do you replace it?  By removing the rotor?<P>||||reg||1
